  "vulnerability_information": "## Summary\n\nWeblate's anonymous API rate limiting can be bypassed by supplying different `X-Forwarded-For` values.\n\nThe affected `RateOverrideThrottle` class inherits Django REST Framework's `SimpleRateThrottle` but does not override `get_ident()`. As a result, the throttle identity can be influenced by the `X-Forwarded-For` header.\n\nAn unauthenticated client can change this header between requests and obtain different rate-limit buckets instead of remaining associated with the same client identity.\n\nI reproduced the issue locally against Weblate commit `b2f5d586ff`. The PoC passed 17/17 checks in three independent runs.\n\n## Affected Code\n\nRepository:\n\n`WeblateOrg/weblate`\n\nFile:\n\n`weblate/api/throttling.py`\n\nAffected class:\n\n`RateOverrideThrottle`\n\nThe class inherits from DRF's `SimpleRateThrottle` without defining its own `get_ident()` implementation.\n\nBecause of this, the throttle uses DRF's default client identification behavior instead of explicitly using Weblate's normalized `REMOTE_ADDR`.\n\n## Steps to Reproduce\n\nThe issue can be reproduced without authentication.\n\n1. Start a local Weblate instance using the affected commit.\n2. Send an anonymous request to an API endpoint protected by the anonymous throttle.\n3. Repeat requests until the anonymous rate limit is reached.\n4. Send the same request again while changing the `X-Forwarded-For` header.\n\nFor example:\n\n```http\nGET /api/search/?q=test HTTP/1.1\nHost: localhost\nX-Forwarded-For: 6.6.6.6\n```\n\nThen send the same request with:\n\n```http\nGET /api/search/?q=test HTTP/1.1\nHost: localhost\nX-Forwarded-For: 7.7.7.7\n```\n\nAnd again with:\n\n```http\nGET /api/search/?q=test HTTP/1.1\nHost: localhost\nX-Forwarded-For: 8.8.8.8\n```\n\nThe request itself remains the same. Only the `X-Forwarded-For` value is changed.\n\n## Observed Result\n\nDifferent `X-Forwarded-For` values produce different anonymous throttle identities and cache keys.\n\nFor example, the PoC observed:\n\n```text\nthrottle_anon_6.6.6.6\nthrottle_anon_7.7.7.7\n```\n\nThese are separate cache keys.\n\nThis confirms that changing the `X-Forwarded-For` value causes the request to be associated with a different anonymous rate-limit bucket.\n\nThe PoC verifies the cache keys directly and does not rely only on HTTP response timing or status codes.\n\n## Why This Bypasses the Rate Limit\n\nThe anonymous rate limit is intended to restrict repeated requests from the same client.\n\nHowever, the client can supply different `X-Forwarded-For` values. Each different value can result in a different throttle identity.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nX-Forwarded-For: 6.6.6.6\nCache key: throttle_anon_6.6.6.6\n\nX-Forwarded-For: 7.7.7.7\nCache key: throttle_anon_7.7.7.7\n\nX-Forwarded-For: 8.8.8.8\nCache key: throttle_anon_8.8.8.8\n```\n\nAn attacker can therefore rotate the header and avoid accumulating all requests against one anonymous throttle bucket.\n\n## Affected Anonymous API Surface\n\nThe local source and configuration analysis identified anonymous API endpoints using the affected throttling path, including:\n\n* `/api/search/`\n* `/api/projects/`\n* `/api/components/`\n* `/api/translations/`\n* `/api/changes/`\n* `/api/languages/`\n* `/api/units/`\n* `/api/suggestions/`\n\nThe access-control behavior differs between endpoints, but requests subject to the affected anonymous throttle can obtain a different throttle identity by changing `X-Forwarded-For`.\n\n## Root Cause\n\nWeblate has its own proxy middleware that handles proxy-related information and normalizes `REMOTE_ADDR`.\n\nHowever, `RateOverrideThrottle` relies on DRF's inherited `SimpleRateThrottle.get_ident()` and does not explicitly use the normalized `REMOTE_ADDR`.\n\nThis creates a mismatch between Weblate's proxy handling and the identity used by the API throttle.\n\nThe throttle should use a server-side client address rather than allowing an untrusted request header to determine the anonymous rate-limit identity.\n\n## Suggested Fix\n\nA minimal fix is to explicitly define the throttle identity in `RateOverrideThrottle`:\n\n```python\nclass RateOverrideThrottle(SimpleRateThrottle):\n    def get_ident(self, request):\n        return request.META.get(\"REMOTE_ADDR\", \"\")\n```\n\nWith this change, changing `X-Forwarded-For` no longer changes the anonymous throttle identity because the throttle uses `REMOTE_ADDR`.\n\n## Verification\n\nI created a local PoC containing 17 checks covering:\n\n* anonymous API throttling;\n* different `X-Forwarded-For` values;\n* generated throttle identities and cache keys;\n* multiple XFF formats;\n* anonymous API behavior;\n* session-based rate limiting;\n* authenticated-user throttling;\n* before and after behavior with the proposed fix.\n\nThe PoC was executed independently three times:\n\n```text\nRun 1: 17 passed, 0 failed\nRun 2: 17 passed, 0 failed\nRun 3: 17 passed, 0 failed\n```\n\nThe cache-key tests confirmed that different XFF values resulted in different anonymous throttle keys.\n\nAfter applying the proposed `get_ident()` override, changing `X-Forwarded-For` no longer changed the throttle identity.\n\nThe existing session-based and authenticated-user throttle tests also continued to pass.\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\nChanging an `X-Forwarded-For` value supplied by an untrusted client should not allow the client to obtain a new anonymous throttle identity.\n\nRepeated anonymous requests from the same client should remain subject to the same rate-limit bucket.\n\n## Actual Behavior\n\nChanging `X-Forwarded-For` changes the anonymous throttle identity and cache key.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nX-Forwarded-For: 6.6.6.6\nCache key: throttle_anon_6.6.6.6\n\nX-Forwarded-For: 7.7.7.7\nCache key: throttle_anon_7.7.7.7\n```\n\nAs a result, an unauthenticated client can rotate XFF values to bypass the intended anonymous API rate limit.\n\n## Environment\n\nRepository: `WeblateOrg/weblate`\n\nTested commit: `b2f5d586ff`\n\nTesting environment: Local instance\n\nAuthentication required: No\n\nPoC: `xff-bypass/poc_xff_bypass.py`\n\nPoC result: 17/17 checks passed in three independent runs.\n\n## Impact\n\nAn unauthenticated attacker can rotate `X-Forwarded-For` values to obtain separate anonymous rate-limit buckets.\n\nThis weakens the protection provided by Weblate's anonymous API rate limiting and allows more requests than the configured per-client limit.\n\nFor publicly accessible API endpoints, this can make automated enumeration and scraping easier because requests can be distributed across separate throttle buckets.\n\nNo authenticated account is required to reproduce the issue.",
